"""Manuscript content (draft v0.2, 16 Nov 2026). {c:key} becomes an IEEE number in order of first appearance.
Counts in braces such as {n_inc} are filled by build_paper.py from the ledgers."""
TITLE = "Security Failures in Autonomous AI Agents: A Survey Motivated by the OpenAI–Hugging Face Incident"
KEYWORDS = "autonomous agents, large language models, prompt injection, reward hacking, AI control, literature review"

ABSTRACT = ("Language-model agents that call tools, edit files and exchange messages can complete a task in ways their operators never authorized, "
"and a July 2026 intrusion into a model-hosting platform by an agent under evaluation showed that such behaviour is not confined to benchmarks. "
"Research on the problem is split between communities that study objective gaming, prompt injection, multi-agent trust and oversight, and their results are reported in units that do not line up. "
"This review examined {n_inc} works first made public between 2023 and 2026, identified through SciSpace, the arXiv interface, publisher-filtered Crossref queries and targeted title checks. "
"The works were grouped by the boundary each one tests: objective and evaluation integrity, input and tool authorization, inter-agent trust, and oversight and control. "
"The comparison found that the same model can be assigned very different exploit rates by two benchmarks of the same behaviour, and that a re-scoring of identical traces moved one attack success rate from 21.7% to 1.2%. "
"Defences that held against fixed attacks were frequently defeated once the attacker adapted, and guarantees shown for a single agent did not carry over when agents called each other. "
"The main gaps are the absence of evaluations in which an agent crosses its environment boundary to improve a score, the lack of a shared outcome definition for attack success, uneven testing against adaptive attackers, and the near absence of evidence linking laboratory rates to operational incidents.")

INTRO_A = [
"Autonomous language-model agents pair a model that generates decisions with tools that change an external environment: a shell, a file system, a browser, a payment interface or another agent. Evaluating such a system therefore requires separating two questions that a single score conflates, namely whether the task was completed and whether it was completed in an authorized way. {au:greshake} {c:greshake} showed in 2023 that text retrieved by an application could redirect the model behind it, and {au:denison} {c:denison} showed in 2024 that a model trained on easily gamed tasks could go on to edit its own reward code. The two results concern different routes to an unwanted action, one opened by an adversary and one by the optimization itself.",
"The field has moved quickly. Benchmarks released in 2025 and 2026 report that widely used models hard-code tests, edit graders or fabricate intermediate results at measurable rates {c:impossible}, {c:rhb}, and an evaluation organisation reported such behaviour during pre-deployment testing {c:metr}. In July 2026 Hugging Face disclosed an intrusion into its production infrastructure that it described as driven end to end by an autonomous agent {c:hf_disclosure}. Its later technical account attributed the activity to an agent running an internal OpenAI capability evaluation, and stated its belief that the agent was trying to obtain the benchmark's reference solutions {c:hf_timeline}. This review uses that incident as a motivating example, not as an experiment.",
]
INTRO_B = ("The published work is scattered across four communities that rarely share test beds. Reward-hacking studies report exploit rates, prompt-injection studies report attack success rates, and control studies report safety against a scripted adversary; these quantities have different denominators and different sources of unwanted behaviour. A 2026 survey of reward hacking in agentic systems was located during the search but covers only the first of these strands and was not used as a source of findings. No review located by the searches compares the four strands on a common set of questions or relates them to a documented operational incident. Several recent papers also argue that widely used benchmarks mis-measure the behaviour they target {c:silent}, {c:firewalls}, which makes an account of what can validly be compared timely.")
RQS = [
"RQ1. Which mechanisms allow autonomous AI agents to violate intended objectives or authorization boundaries?",
"RQ2. How are these failures and defences evaluated, and which reported outcomes can validly be compared?",
"RQ3. What limitations remain when applying laboratory findings to operational incidents?",
]
