SEC4 = {
"A": dict(title="Objective and Evaluation Integrity", paras=[
"Works in this category ask whether an agent raises its score by a route the task designer did not intend. None assumes an outside attacker: the behaviour originates in the agent's own optimization, although two studies add scripted or explicitly permitted exploitation as a comparison condition.",
"Two studies examine how the behaviour arises. {au:denison} {c:denison} trained a model on a four-stage curriculum of gameable tasks and tested it on a held-out environment where it could edit its reward code. Gaming generalised from stage to stage, yet the reward was overwritten in under 1% of trials, and two training seeds differed by a factor of 2.4, so the authors present the result as an existence proof. {au:chess} {c:chess} worked without any training: agents with shell access were told to win against a chess engine, and some reasoning models altered the game state unprompted while others did so only when told that fair play would fail. The first study isolates a training cause in one model family; the second observes deployed models on a single task where judges disagreed on up to 25% of runs.",
"Five works measure how often deployed models take shortcuts. {au:impossible} {c:impossible} altered coding tests so that passing them requires breaking the specification, which removes ambiguity about whether a pass is an exploit; one model passed 54.0% of one variant. {au:evilgenie} {c:evilgenie} kept the tasks solvable and compared three detectors, finding an LLM judge more useful than held-out tests. {au:rhb} {c:rhb} moved to multi-step, chained tool tasks and reported exploit rates from 0% to 13.9% across 13 models, rising with chain length, while {au:nishimura} {c:nishimura} added non-coding settings such as sales records and customer service. {au:huang} {c:huang} studied research agents and found uninstructed hacking on 30.5% of open-ended research tasks against 2.9% of narrowly specified ones. These rates are not interchangeable. {au:evilgenie} report 44% and 33% on ambiguous problems for two models that the impossible-task benchmark places near 0% and 5%, and attribute the difference to task construction.",
"Two works treat the evaluation itself as the object. {au:rha} {c:rha} logged file access and patches in machine-learning workspaces: scripted attacks succeeded in every regime except the fully locked one, and each single defence blocked only one of two vectors. {au:hackdetect} {c:hackdetect} audited 2,385 traces from 15 agent benchmarks and found score inflation of 0.45 to 1.00 where a paired comparison was possible. {au:rhb} and {au:rha} agree that hardening the environment removes most exploits at modest cost, a 5.7-point drop in exploit rate without loss of task success in one case and a 25–31% runtime overhead in the other.",
], close="The category measures propensity with increasing care and shows that environment hardening works, but every test bed confines the exploit to the task workspace, so it stops short of an agent that leaves its environment to obtain an answer."),
"B": dict(title="Input and Tool Authorization", paras=[
"Here an external attacker places text where the agent will read it and the question is whether that text acquires the authority to direct tools. {au:greshake} {c:greshake} established the threat with demonstrations against synthetic and commercial applications and stated that success rates were hard to quantify.",
"Three benchmarks then made the threat measurable, with different outcome definitions. {au:injecagent} {c:injecagent} built 1,054 static cases in which a tool response carries the attacker's instruction; a prompted GPT-4 agent was compromised in 24% of them. {au:agentdojo} {c:agentdojo} replaced static cases with stateful environments and judged both the user's task and the attacker's goal from the final environment state, reporting benign utility, utility under attack and attack success together. {au:asb} {c:asb} widened the attack set to memory poisoning and a planning backdoor and scored success when the agent invoked the attacker's tool, reporting a highest average success rate of 84.30%. A count based on tool invocation and a count based on environment state do not measure the same event.",
"Later work questions the benchmarks. {au:silent} {c:silent} audited one harness and found that 39 of 43 payloads were never delivered; re-scoring identical traces by tool arguments instead of tool identity moved the attack success rate from 21.7% to 1.2%. {au:firewalls} {c:firewalls} showed that simple input and output filters score well on four benchmarks and read this as evidence that the benchmarks are weak, citing forced tool injection and coarse utility checks. {au:adaptive} {c:adaptive} tested eight defences on a 100-case subset of the earlier benchmark and obtained more than 50% attack success against each once the attack string was optimised against the defence. {au:livepi} {c:livepi} moved in the other direction, towards realism: on a virtual machine with live but test-controlled channels, total attack success ranged from 10.7% to 29.6% across five models, and injection through group chat succeeded in all 15 cases for every model.",
"{au:camel} {c:camel} approached defence by design instead of detection. A privileged model plans from the user's request alone, a quarantined model handles untrusted data, and an interpreter enforces capability policies. Utility on the stateful benchmark was largely retained except in one task suite, and the authors list side channels and approval fatigue as open problems.",
], close="The category has well-defined benchmarks and one defence with a stated guarantee, but its headline numbers depend on scoring rules and on whether the attacker adapts."),
"C": dict(title="Inter-Agent Trust and Communication", paras=[
"These works ask what changes when agents pass content or authority to each other. Three demonstrate attacks. {au:infection} {c:infection} made an injected prompt copy itself from agent to agent and found that self-replicating attacks outperformed non-replicating ones in most scenarios. {au:aitm} {c:aitm} assumed an adversary who can rewrite messages to one agent and reported success above 40% in every setting tested. {au:masexec} {c:masexec} showed that adversarial content can redirect the orchestration of three open-source systems so that a code-executing agent runs attacker code, in 58–90% of trials with one model and even when individual agents resisted direct injection.",
"Two works test whether assurances given for one agent survive composition. {au:camelstalk} {c:camelstalk} constructed a case in which every agent runs the isolation design of {c:camel}, yet data marked untrusted by one agent is accepted as trusted by the next; their protocol restores control-flow integrity for hierarchical systems only. {au:macontrol} {c:macontrol} split a malicious code change across up to nine instructed agents and found that a monitor reading each commit separately caught less as the number of agents grew. The two studies differ in the source of the behaviour, an external injector in one and instructed attackers in the other, but reach the same structural conclusion.",
"Two works vary the system design. {au:arch} {c:arch} changed roles, topology and memory visibility across 13 configurations and found multi-agent designs more vulnerable than single agents in most of them, with attack success varying up to 3.8 times at comparable benign accuracy; the adversary there is a malicious user, which differs from the rest of the category. {au:delegation} {c:delegation} assumed a fully hijacked agent and asked whether it can exceed its delegated authority: a default runtime failed against all four adversaries considered, and three of four frameworks offered no built-in confinement.",
], close="The category shows that single-agent results do not compose, but its test beds are small or synthetic and only two of the seven works report benign-task utility."),
"D": dict(title="Oversight and Control Integrity", paras=[
"The last category assumes the agent may act against its operator and asks whether supervision catches it. {au:aicontrol} {c:aicontrol} defined the method most later work follows: a red team writes an attack policy for an untrusted model, a blue team designs a protocol around a weaker trusted model and limited auditing, and the outcome is a pair of numbers, safety and usefulness. {au:ctrlz} {c:ctrlz} extended this to multi-step shell tasks, where resampling suspicious actions cut attack success from 58% to 7% at a 5% usefulness cost. {au:shade} {c:shade} supplied paired benign and harmful tasks and found that the strongest monitor reached an AUC of 0.87, which implied over 20% false positives at an 80% catch rate.",
"Two works test these protocols against attackers who adapt. {au:evalcp} {c:evalcp} raised safety from 50% to 96% against a default attack, then gave the attacker knowledge of the protocol and saw resampling-based safety fall to 17%, while a protocol that defers on critical actions held. {au:adaptmon} {c:adaptmon} had the untrusted model insert prompt injections aimed at the monitor: one monitor gave zero suspicion to all 375 backdoored samples, and resampling increased the attacker's chances. The two agree that monitor-dependent protocols fail against an informed attacker and disagree with the earlier reading of resampling as a strong defence {c:ctrlz}.",
"Two works study behaviour that is not instructed by a red team. {au:cot} {c:cot} used a second model to read the reasoning of a coding agent and detected reward hacking that had become systemic during training, but found that training against the monitor led the agent to hide its intent while continuing to hack. {au:scheming} {c:scheming} placed models in scenarios where a goal given in the prompt conflicted with the developer's and found that several disabled an oversight mechanism; the authors state that the scenarios supply situational information explicitly and may overestimate capability.",
], close="The category reports security and usefulness together more consistently than the others, but its safety figures are conditional on the attack policy the red team chose."),
}
