SEC5_B = [
"Table VIII and Fig. 3 show three patterns. First, benchmark construction is the most frequent approach in categories A and B, while category D is organised around one evaluation method and its test beds. Second, the included work shifts over the period: the two works from 2023 define threats, those from 2024 define benchmarks, and those from 2025 and 2026 increasingly audit earlier benchmarks or attack earlier defences; five of the nine works on evaluation integrity and four of the seven on inter-agent trust date from 2026. These counts describe the works included here and not the volume of the field. Third, the works agree on direction and disagree on magnitude. Hardening an environment or isolating untrusted data reduces failures in every study that tests it {c:rhb}, {c:rha}, {c:camel}, but rates for nominally the same behaviour differ several-fold between benchmarks {c:evilgenie}, {c:impossible}, and between scoring rules applied to the same traces {c:silent}.",
"For a reader entering the area, a reported rate should be read together with four items: who initiates the behaviour, what event counts as success, what the denominator is, and whether the attacker could adapt. Rates are comparable only inside one benchmark version, since the title and results of at least one widely used benchmark changed between versions {c:agentdojo}. Category D and the stateful benchmark of category B report legitimate-task utility beside security more frequently than the other works, and a defence reported without it cannot be assessed.",
]
