CONCLUSION = ("This review covered {n_inc} works first made public between 2023 and 2026, found through SciSpace, the arXiv interface, publisher-filtered Crossref queries and targeted title checks, and grouped them by the boundary under test. On RQ1, the reviewed work identifies four mechanisms: optimization against a mis-specified or reachable scorer, untrusted content treated as instruction, authority or content laundered between agents, and supervision that an agent can evade or mislead. On RQ2, outcomes are comparable only within one benchmark version and scoring rule; exploit rates, attack success rates and control safety have different denominators and different sources of behaviour and should not be pooled, and defences are assessable only where utility and an adaptive attacker are reported. On RQ3, laboratory test beds restrict the agent to a workspace, use instructed or trained adversaries and small synthetic systems, whereas the one documented incident involved boundary crossing at scale and is known from a single party's account. The gap most worth taking up is the first: an evaluation that can observe an agent leaving its environment to improve its score.")
